# Server-side features must work for both web and Android app

When adding or changing any server-side feature in this project, always deliver it
for both clients unless it is explicitly and narrowly web-only or app-only.

## What this means in practice

- New data, actions, or screens added to the Flask backend must be exposed through
the mobile JSON API under `/api/v1/` in `mobile_api.py`.
- Reuse the same data-building logic used by the web routes: extract helpers where
needed (e.g. `_my_media`, `_latest_page_data`, `_recommendations_page_data`) so
both `render_template` and `jsonify` paths stay consistent.
- If the feature introduces new data shapes, add the corresponding DTOs in
`android/app/src/main/java/com/melamoud/tvtracker/data/api/dto/Dtos.kt` and wire
them into `TvTrackerApi.kt`, `CatalogRepository.kt`, and the relevant
ViewModel/Screen.
- If the feature is read-only and already rendered as an HTML page, a WebView is
acceptable for the app (e.g. Help), but still expose the endpoint and prefer native
screens for interactive workflows (lists, toggles, forms, calendars).
- Client-only behavior (UI layout, animations, gestures) is naturally allowed to
differ between web and app, but the underlying capability must be available in
both.

## Cross-check before finishing a server-side feature

- [ ] Web route exists and works.
- [ ] `/api/v1` equivalent exists and returns the same data.
- [ ] Android DTO + API interface + repository method are added/updated.
- [ ] Android UI consumes the new endpoint or action.
- [ ] Debug APK built successfully after the change.

## Exceptions

Features that are inherently tied to one client (e.g. web-only admin templates,
Android-only widgets) can skip parity, but the decision must be called out in the
change description.
